			"color":"#322",
			"widgets_values":[
				"Subject:\n- Subject 1: the exact pink magical wand from Picture 1, with a circular pink frame, central gold star, white wings, pink gemstones, gold ornaments, a red central jewel, and the vertical gold text “SAKURA” on the handle. Preserve its proportions, colors, materials, lettering, wing shape, gem placement, and overall design throughout.\n- Subject 2: the exact young athletic woman from Picture 2, with the same facial identity, blonde tied-back hair, body proportions,  matching leggings, natural skin texture, and calm confident presence.\n\nPicture:\n- Picture 1 is the appearance reference for Subject 1.\n- Picture 2 is the appearance reference for Subject 2 and the exact outdoor setting: a sunlit private garden with green trees, a wooden fence, warm late-afternoon backlight, and natural vegetation.\n\nVideo:\n- No reference video. Create one continuous transformation sequence in which Subject 2 notices Subject 1 while exercising, picks it up, activates it, and completes a magical transformation.\n\nAudio:\n- No reference audio. Use natural outdoor ambience, subtle breathing, fabric movement, footsteps, a crystalline magical activation sound, rising energy tones, and one clean transformation impact. No dialogue, no narration, no subtitles, and no additional on-screen text.\n\nVisual:\nCreate a cinematic magical-girl transformation scene grounded in a realistic outdoor workout setting. Begin naturally and gradually introduce controlled pink-and-gold fantasy effects.\n\n[Shot 1]\nSubject 2 performs a slow standing side stretch in the same garden shown in Picture 2. She extends one arm across her chest, breathes steadily, and shifts her weight naturally. Warm sunlight outlines her shoulders and hair. As she finishes the stretch, a brief pink reflection flickers across the ground beside a patch of grass.\n\nShe notices the unusual light. Her eyes move first, then her head turns toward it. She relaxes her arms, takes one cautious step closer, and looks down with a curious expression.\n\n[Shot 2] 2.8s\nReveal Subject 1 lying partially hidden in the grass, still preserving the exact design from Picture 1. The central red jewel emits a soft pulse while the white wings make one subtle mechanical flutter.\n\nSubject 2 bends down, reaches toward the wand, and pauses just before touching it. The jewel pulses again. She then wraps her fingers around the handle and lifts it from the ground. Grass blades bend under the movement and settle after the wand is removed.\n\n[Shot 3] 5.0s\nAs Subject 2 raises the wand in front of her chest, the central jewel activates. A thin ribbon of pink-and-gold light travels from the jewel around the circular frame and central star, then spirals around her wrist, arm, and body.\n\nThe transformation follows visible causality: the jewel lights up, energy flows into her hand, luminous ribbons circle her body, her sportswear dissolves into particles, and a refined pink-and-white magical outfit forms progressively from her torso outward. The new outfit includes a fitted white bodice, pink accents, a short layered skirt, elegant gloves, light boots, and small gold star details. Preserve her facial identity, hairstyle, body proportions, and realistic skin throughout.\n\n[Shot 4] 7.6s\nThe transformation completes as the final ribbon of light forms a short cape-like back detail and fades into sparkling particles. Her feet settle firmly onto the ground. Her hair and new costume respond naturally to the remaining energy and then come to rest.\n\nShe turns toward the camera, raises the wand beside her face, and adopts a confident three-quarter pose. The wand’s star and red jewel glow softly while the garden remains visible behind her. She gives a restrained smile and holds the final heroic composition until the end.\n\nCamera:\nUse physically coherent cinematic camera movement with clear spatial continuity. Begin with a medium-wide shot matching Picture 2. Follow her gaze with a gentle tilt and lateral move to reveal the wand. During the pickup, move closer to clearly show preparation, hand contact, grip, lifting, and settling. During the transformation, make one smooth clockwise orbit combined with a gradual push-in, keeping her full body and the complete wand visible.\n\nEnd in a medium hero shot showing her transformed costume, face, and wand together. Preserve Subject 1’s exact design, Subject 2’s identity, the garden layout, sunlight direction, and object scale. Use realistic fabric dynamics, hair movement, shadows, reflections, and ground contact.\n\nAvoid abrupt cuts, instant costume replacement, body morphing, facial drift, extra limbs, distorted hands, duplicated wand parts, unreadable “SAKURA” text, oversized magical explosions, chaotic particles, floating feet, loss of background continuity, or overly sexualized posing."
			],
